    "question": "Sophia ate 1/6 of her pie and she put the rest on the fridge. If the pie left in the fridge weighs 1200 grams, how many grams did Sophia eat?",
    "gold": "240",
    "answer": "If Sophia ate 1/6 of the pie, then 6/6 - 1/6 = 5/6 is left in the fridge.\nLet x be the pie's original weight.\nThe current weight of the pie can be described by 5x/6=1200 grams\nSo, 5x=7200.\nAnd, the original weight is x = <<1440=1440>>1440 grams.\nSo, Sophia ate 1440 grams original - 1200 grams after= <<1440-1200=240>>240 grams of pie.\n#### 240"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many grams of pie Sophia ate, we start by understanding the relationship between the fraction of the pie she ate and the weight of the pie left in the fridge. We know that Sophia ate \\( \\frac{1}{6} \\) of the pie, which means the remaining \\( \\frac{5}{6} \\) of the pie is left in the fridge. We are given that the weight of the pie left in the fridge is 1200 grams. Therefore, we can set up the following equation to find the total weight of the pie:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{6} \\times \\text{total weight of the pie} = 1200 \\text{ grams}\n\\]\n\nTo find the total weight of the pie, we solve for the total weight by isolating it on one side of the equation. We do this by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of \\( \\frac{5}{6} \\), which is \\( \\frac{6}{5} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{total weight of the pie} = 1200 \\text{ grams} \\times \\frac{6}{5}\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\text{total weight of the pie} = 1200 \\times \\frac{6}{5} = 1200 \\times 1.2 = 1440 \\text{ grams}\n\\]\n\nThe total weight of the pie is 1440 grams. Since Sophia ate \\( \\frac{1}{6} \\) of the pie, we calculate the weight of the pie she ate by multiplying the total weight by \\( \\frac{1}{6} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{weight of the pie Sophia ate} = 1440 \\text{ grams} \\times \\frac{1}{6} = 240 \\text{ grams}\n\\]\n\nThus, the weight of the pie that Sophia ate is \\(\\boxed{240}\\) grams.",
